   "q": "How does the CKY algorithm parse a sentence, and what is its complexity?",
   "hint": "Fill a triangle of spans from short to long.",
   "answer": "CKY needs a grammar in Chomsky normal form, where every rule is A → B C or A → word. It fills a table where cell (i, j) holds every non terminal that can produce words i to j. Short spans come first. For each span it tries every split point k and every rule A → B C with B in (i, k) and C in (k, j). The sentence is grammatical if S is in the cell covering everything. Cost O(n³ |G|).",
   "explain": "<p>CKY is to parsing what Viterbi is to tagging: dynamic programming that shares sub results. With a PCFG, keeping the best probability per cell gives the most likely parse, and summing gives the inside probability.</p>",
   "points": [
    "Chomsky normal form",
    "Spans from short to long",
    "O(n³ |G|)"
   ]
