   "q": "How is BLEU computed, and what are its limits?",
   "hint": "Clipped n-gram precision and a brevity penalty.",
   "answer": "BLEU compares a candidate translation with one or more references. For n = 1 to 4 it computes modified precision, counting each candidate n-gram at most as often as it appears in a reference (clipping). It takes the geometric mean of the four precisions and multiplies by a brevity penalty, BP = 1 if the candidate is longer than the reference and exp(1 − r ÷ c) otherwise. Limits: it ignores meaning and synonyms, is unreliable on single sentences, and correlates only loosely with human judgement for strong systems.",
   "explain": "<p>Candidate \"the the the the the the the\" against reference \"the cat is on the mat\" has unigram precision 7/7 without clipping but 2/7 with it, since \"the\" appears only twice in the reference.</p>",
   "points": [
    "Clipped n-gram precision, n = 1 to 4",
    "Geometric mean",
    "Brevity penalty"
   ]
